ve is blue, then he is white. Which is already true, so no conflict.\n\nFrom point 15: If Dave is white and blue, then he is green. So, since Dave is both white and blue, he must be green.\n\nNow, from point 11: All nice, green people are young. Since Dave is nice and green, he must be young.\n\nFrom point 12: If someone is green, they are quiet. So, Dave is quiet.\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\n- Dave is white (point 6).\n- Therefore, Dave is nice (points 9,10).\n- Nice people are kind (point 13), so Dave is kind.\n- Since Dave is kind and white, he is blue (point 8).\n- Since Dave is blue, he is white (point 14) \u2013 already known.\n- Since Dave is white and blue, he is green (point 15).\n- Since Dave is green, he is quiet (point 12).\n- Since Dave is nice and green, he is young (point 11).\n\nSo, all these attributes are consistent. Therefore, Dave is blue.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any contradiction. For example, point 15 says if Dave is white and blue, then he is green. So, Dave is green. But point 1 says Anne is green, and point 4 says Charlie is green. So, multiple people can be green, which is fine.\n\nPoint 12 says if someone is green, they are quiet. So, Dave is quiet, which is fine.\n\nPoint 11 says all nice, green people are young. So, Dave is young, which is fine.\n\nSo, there's no contradiction. Therefore, Dave is blue.\n\nSo, the statement \"Dave is blue\" is true. Therefore, the correct answer is A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A",
