final_output:  ['Wait, but I\'m getting a bit confused. Let me try to outline the logical steps.\n\n1. From statement 10: The cat likes the rabbit. So, the cat has positive feelings towards the rabbit.\n\n2. From statement 21: If the rabbit likes the cat and the cat is round, then the rabbit is blue. We know the rabbit is round (statement 11). So, if the rabbit likes the cat, then the rabbit is blue.\n\n3. Do we know if the rabbit is blue? Let\'s check other statements.\n\n4. Statement 16: If someone eats the rabbit and the rabbit is cold, then they are blue. But we don\'t know if the rabbit is cold or if someone eats the rabbit.\n\n5. Statement 19: If someone eats the rabbit and they are blue, then they eat the bald eagle. Again, we don\'t know if someone eats the rabbit or if they are blue.\n\n6. Statement 17: If the rabbit sees the cat and the rabbit is round, then the cat eats the rabbit. We know the rabbit is round, but do we know if the rabbit sees the cat? Statement 12 says the rabbit sees the bald eagle, but not the cat. So, unless the rabbit sees the cat, this doesn\'t apply.\n\n7. Statement 6: The bald eagle sees the cat. So, the cat is visible to the bald eagle, but that doesn\'t mean the rabbit sees the cat.\n\n8. Statement 15: If someone is young and they eat the rabbit, then the rabbit sees the cat. The bald eagle is young (statement 4) and eats the rabbit (statement 3). So, according to statement 15, if the bald eagle is young and eats the rabbit, then the rabbit sees the cat. Therefore, the rabbit sees the cat.\n\nSo, from statement 15, since the bald eagle is young and eats the rabbit, the rabbit sees the cat. Therefore, the rabbit sees the cat.\n\nNow, going back to statement 17: If the rabbit sees the cat and the rabbit is round, then the cat eats the rabbit. We know the rabbit sees the cat (from statement 15) and the rabbit is round (statement 11). Therefore, the cat eats the rabbit.\n\nBut statement 9 says the cat eats the bald eagle, not the rabbit. So, is there a contradiction here? Or does the cat eat both the bald eagle and the rabbit?\n\nWait, statement 9 says the cat eats the bald eagle. Statement 17, based on the rabbit seeing the cat and being round, leads to the cat eating the rabbit. So, the cat eats both the bald eagle and the rabbit.\n\nBut that\'s possible. So, the cat eats the bald eagle and the rabbit.\n\nNow, going back to statement 21: If the rabbit likes the cat and the cat is round, then the rabbit is blue. We know the rabbit is round, but do we know if the rabbit is blue?\n\nFrom statement 16: If someone eats the rabbit and the rabbit is cold, then they are blue. But we don\'t know if the rabbit is cold or if someone eats the rabbit.\n\nFrom statement 19: If someone eats the rabbit and they are blue, then they eat the bald eagle. Again, we don\'t know if someone eats the rabbit or if they are blue.\n\nBut from statement 17, we know the cat eats the rabbit. So, the cat is the one who eats the rabbit. Therefore, in statement 16, the subject is "someone" who eats the rabbit. Since the cat eats the rabbit, let\'s apply statement 16: If the cat eats the rabbit and the rabbit is cold, then the cat is blue. But we don\'t know if the rabbit is cold.\n\nSimilarly, statement 19: If the cat eats the rabbit and the cat is blue, then the cat eats the bald eagle. But we know the cat already eats the bald eagle (statement 9), so if the cat is blue, then it eats the bald eagle, which it already does. So, that doesn\'t add new information.\n\nBut we don\'t know if the rabbit is cold or if the cat is blue. So, we can\'t determine the rabbit\'s color from this.\n\nWait, but statement 21 says if the rabbit likes the cat and the cat is round, then the rabbit is blue. We know the rabbit is round, but do we know if the cat is round? Statement 11 says the rabbit is round, but nothing about the cat. So, we don\'t know if the cat is round.\n\nTherefore, statement 21\'s condition is only partially met. We know the rabbit is round, but we don\'t know if the cat is round. So, we can\'t conclude whether the rabbit is blue or not based on statement 21.\n\nSo, going back to the original question: Does the rabbit like the cat?\n\nFrom statement 21, if the rabbit likes the cat and the cat is round, then the rabbit is blue. But since we don\'t know if the cat is round or if the rabbit is blue, we can\'t directly infer the rabbit\'s feelings.\n\nHowever, from statement 10, the cat likes the rabbit. Does that imply anything about the rabbit\'s feelings? Not necessarily. Just because the cat likes the rabbit doesn\'t mean the rabbit likes the cat.\n\nBut let\'s think about statement 20: If someone likes the cat, then the cat is round. We know the cat is round (statement 11). So, if someone likes the cat, then the cat is round. But the cat is already round, so that doesn\'t necessarily mean someone likes the cat.\n\nWait, statement 20 is a conditional: If someone likes the cat, then the cat is round. It doesn\'t say that if the cat is round, then someone likes the cat. So, the cat being round doesn\'t imply that someone likes the cat.\n\nSo, statement 20 doesn\'t help us determine if the rabbit likes the cat.\n\nAnother angle: statement 17 tells us that the cat eats the rabbit because the rabbit sees the cat and is round. So, the cat eats the rabbit. Now, if the rabbit likes the cat, then according to statement 21, the rabbit would be blue. But we don\'t know if the rabbit is blue.\n\nBut let\'s see if the rabbit is blue. From statement 16: If someone eats the rabbit and the rabbit is cold, then they are blue. The cat eats the rabbit, so if the rabbit is cold, the cat is blue. But we don\'t know if the rabbit is cold.\n\nFrom statement 19: If someone eats the rabbit and they are blue, then they eat the bald eagle. The cat eats the rabbit, so if the cat is blue, then the cat eats the bald eagle, which it already does. So, that doesn\'t help.\n\nSo, we don\'t know if the rabbit is cold or if the cat is blue. Therefore, we can\'t determine the rabbit\'s color, which is tied to whether the rabbit likes the cat via statement 21.\n\nBut wait, statement 21 says if the rabbit likes the cat and the cat is round, then the rabbit is blue. We know the rabbit is round, but we don\'t know if the cat is round. So, even if the rabbit likes the cat, unless the cat is round, the rabbit wouldn\'t necessarily be blue. But since the rabbit is round, and the cat\'s roundness is unknown, we can\'t conclude.\n\nSo, putting it all together, we don\'t have enough information to determine whether the rabbit likes the cat or not. Therefore, the statement "The rabbit does not like the cat" is unknown.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought that statement 15 leads to the rabbit seeing the cat, which then via statement 17 leads to the cat eating the rabbit. So, the cat eats the rabbit. Now, if the rabbit likes the cat, then according to statement 21, the rabbit would be blue. But we don\'t know if the rabbit is blue. So, if the rabbit is not blue, then it might not like the cat. But we don\'t know the rabbit\'s color.\n\nAlternatively, if the rabbit is blue, then it might like the cat. But again, we don\'t know.\n\nSo, without knowing the rabbit\'s color, we can\'t determine its feelings towards the cat. Therefore, the statement is unknown.\n\nWait, but let me think again. From statement 15, the rabbit sees the cat. From statement 17, since the rabbit sees the cat and is round, the cat eats the rabbit. So, the cat eats the rabbit. Now, if the rabbit likes the cat, then according to statement 21, the rabbit is blue. But if the rabbit is not blue, then it doesn\'t like the cat. So, if we can determine the rabbit\'s color, we can know.\n\nBut do we have any information about the rabbit\'s color? Let\'s check.\n\nStatement 11: The rabbit is round. That\'s about shape, not color.\n\nStatement 16: If someone eats the rabbit and the rabbit is cold, then they are blue. But we don\'t know if t