een, so if Charlie is also blue, then Charlie is not big. But that's acceptable because we don't have any information that Charlie is big.\n\nWait, but fact 15 says all green, blue people are not big. So if Charlie is green and blue, then Charlie is not big. But we don't know if Charlie is big or not. So that's okay.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that Charlie is blue, so the statement is true.\n\nWait, but I'm getting conflicting conclusions. Let me try to structure this more clearly.\n\n1. Charlie is green (fact 4).\n\n2. All green people are nice (fact 12). Therefore, Charlie is nice.\n\n3. All nice people are blue (fact 17). Therefore, Charlie is blue.\n\n4. Therefore, Charlie is blue.\n\nBut fact 15 says all green, blue people are not big. So since Charlie is green and blue, Charlie is not big.\n\nBut is that a problem? No, because we don't have any information that Charlie is big. So that's acceptable.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Charlie is blue\" is true.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought Charlie is not blue because of fact 15, but that's only if Charlie is both green and blue, which would make Charlie not big. But that doesn't mean Charlie can't be blue. It just means that if Charlie is both green and blue, then Charlie is not big. But Charlie could still be blue without being green, but in this case, Charlie is green, so if Charlie is also blue, then Charlie is not big. But that's okay because we don't have any information that Charlie is big.\n\nSo, the conclusion is that Charlie is blue, so the statement is true.\n\nWait, but I'm still a bit confused because fact 15 seems to imply that if someone is green and blue, they are not big, but that doesn't prevent them from being blue. It just adds another attribute.\n\nSo, in conclusion, the statement \"Charlie is blue\" is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A) True\n\nCharlie is green, which makes him nice, and since all nice people are blue, Charlie must be blue.",
