ound.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the cat is round.\n\nWait, but is this chain of reasoning invalid? Let me check each step.\n\nStep 1: Cat avoids squirrel → Cat is smooth (statement 17). That\'s incorrect.\n\nStep 2: Smooth → warm (statement 13). Incorrect.\n\nStep 3: Warm → avoid cow (statement 18). So, cat avoids cow.\n\nStep 4: Avoiding cow → not see rabbit (statement 16). So, cat does not see rabbit.\n\nStep 5: Not seeing rabbit → round (statement 14). So, cat is round.\n\nYes, that seems to not hold. So, the cat is round.\n\nAlternatively, is there any possibility that the cat is not round? Let me see. The only non-round entity mentioned is the rabbit (statement 5). There’s a statement that says the cat is not round. So, unless there’s a rule that says all animals are not round except the rabbit, but that’s not stated. So, the only way the cat could be not round is if there’s a rule that says so, but there isn’t. So, based on the reasoning above, the cat is round.\n\nTherefore, the statement "The cat is round" is false.\n\n']]
