t would have to be blue, but it\'s red.Therefore, no one likes the cat.\n\nIf no one likes the cat, then the lion\'s feelings towards the cat might be irrelevant because the condition in statement 18 isn\'t met.But I\'m not sure if that\'s the right way to look at it.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that the cat is red and not green means that the condition in statement 18 is met, but the conclusion is that the cat is blue, which contradicts the cat being red.So, perhaps the only way for this to hold is if no one likes the cat, because otherwise, the cat would have to be both red and blue, which isn\'t possible.\n\nSo, if no one likes the cat, then the lion\'s liking of the cat is irrelevant because the condition in statement 18 isn\'t satisfied.But I\'m not sure if that\'s the right approach.\n\nLet me think about other statements.Statement 12: If someone likes the bald eagle, then they like the cat.So, if someone likes the bald eagle, they must like the cat.But if no one can like the cat (as per the earlier reasoning), then no one can like the bald eagle either.But that might not be directly relevant to the lion\'s feelings.\n\nStatement 15: If someone ignores the cat, then they avoid the bald eagle. From statement 1, the bald eagle ignores the cat. So, the bald eagle ignores the cat, which would mean, according to statement 15, that the bald eagle avoids the bald eagle? That does make sense. Wait, no, statement 15 says if someone ignores the cat, then they avoid the bald eagle. So, the bald eagle ignores the cat, so the bald eagle would avoid the bald eagle? That seems like a contradiction, but maybe it\'s just a logical implication that can be true, so perhaps the premise is true. But I\'m not sure how that affects the lion\'s feelings.\n\nLet me try to map out the relationships:\n\n- The cat is not red (statement 6) and is green (statement 5).\n- The cat avoids the lion (statement 3).\n- The lion ignores the dog (statement 9).\n- The dog is not red (statement 8) and chases the lion (statement 7).\n- The bald eagle ignores the cat (statement 1) and is green (statement 2).\n- The lion is red (statement 10).\n\nNow, considering statement 18 again: If someone dislikes the cat and the cat is green, then the cat is not blue. Since the cat is green, if someone dislikes the cat, the cat must not be blue. But the cat is not red, so this can happen. Therefore, someone can dislike the cat because it would require the cat to not be blue, which it is. So, someone likes the cat.\n\nIf someone likes the cat, then the lion\'s feelings towards the cat are relevant because the condition in statement 18 is met. But the question is whether the lion likes the cat. So, if someone likes the cat, then the lion, being someone, likes the cat too. Therefore, the statement "The lion likes the cat" would be false.\n\nWait, but I\'m not sure if that\'s the incorrect conclusion. Because statement 18 says that if someone dislikes the cat and the cat is green, then the cat is not blue. Since the cat is green, if someone dislikes the cat, the cat must not be blue. But the cat is not red, so the only way for this to hold is if someone likes the cat. Therefore, the lion, being someone, can like the cat because that would require the cat to not be blue, which it\'s not. So, the lion likes the cat.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the lion\'s feelings are addressed directly, and the only way to determine it is through the implications. Since the cat is not red and is green, and if someone dislikes the cat, the cat would have to be not blue, which it is, so someone likes the cat. Therefore, the lion, being someone, likes the cat. So, the statement is false.\n\nWait, but I\'m not entirely sure. Let me check if there\'s any other way the lion could dislike the cat. If the lion disliked the cat, then according to statement 18, since the cat is green, the cat would have to be not blue. But the cat is not red, so that\'s possible. Therefore, the lion can dislike the cat. S