is not young.So if Bob is young, then the statement "Bob is not young" would be false.But let me check if there\'s any contradiction or if I\'m missing something.\n\nWait, let\'s see if there\'s any other information about Bob.The only facts about Bob are that he\'s quiet and white.So from that, we can deduce he\'s round (from fact 11) and therefore young (from fact 14).So Bob is young, which would make the statement "Bob is not young" false.\n\nBut let me make sure I\'m not missing any other rules that might affect this.Let\'s look at the other facts to see if they impact Bob\'s status.\n\nFact 13 says all young things are kind.So if Bob is young, he must be kind.But do we have any information about Bob being kind?The facts don\'t mention Bob being kind, but since he\'s young, he must be kind.However, that doesn\'t directly affect the question about his age.\n\nFact 15 says all quiet, kind things are white.Bob is quiet and, if he\'s young, he\'s kind.So he fits the criteria of being quiet and kind, which makes him white, which aligns with fact 2.So that\'s consistent.\n\nFact 17 says young, quiet things are kind.Bob is young (from earlier deduction) and quiet, so he must be kind.Again, consistent.\n\nFact 18 says all quiet, red things are kind.But Bob is white, not red, so this doesn\'t apply to him.\n\nFact 16 says quiet, green things are red.But Bob is white, so this doesn\'t apply either.\n\nFact 12 says if something is kind and white, then it\'s green.Bob is kind (since he\'s young) and white, so according to this, he should be green.But wait, Bob is white, not green.That seems contradictory.So if Bob is kind and white, he should be green, but he\'s white.That\'s a problem.\n\nWait, that\'s a contradiction.Let me check that again.Fact 12: If something is kind and white, then it is green.So if Bob is kind and white, he must be green.But Bob is white, not green.So that would mean Bob is not kind.But earlier, from fact 13, all young things are kind, and from fact 14, Bob is young, so he must be kind.So this creates a contradiction.\n\nHmm, that\'s confusing.Let me try to untangle this.\n\nIf Bob is white, then from fact 11, he\'s round.From fact 14, he\'s young.From fact 13, he\'s kind.So Bob is kind and white, which according to fact 12, should make him green.But Bob is white, not green.So that\'s a contradiction.Therefore, our initial assumption that Bob is young must be wrong.\n\nWait, but how?Because fact 14 says all round things are young, and Bob is round because he\'s white.So he must be young.But then he\'s kind and white, which makes him green, but he\'s not green.So this is a paradox.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I\'m misapplying the rules.Let me go through each step again.\n\n1.Bob is quiet (fact 1) and white (fact 2).\n2.From fact 11, white things are round, so Bob is round.\n3.From fact 14, all round things are young, so Bob is young.\n4.From fact 13, all young things are kind, so Bob is kind.\n5.Now, from fact 12, if something is kind and white, it\'s green.So Bob is kind and white, so he should be green.\n6.But Bob is white, not green.So this is a contradiction.\n\nThis suggests that our initial assumption that Bob is young is incorrect, but according to the facts, he must be young because he\'s round.So perhaps the only way to resolve this is that Bob cannot be both white and kind, but that\'s not possible because he\'s young, so he must be kind.\n\nWait, maybe the problem is that the rules are conflicting, so the conclusion is that Bob cannot exist under these rules, but that\'s not an option here.Alternatively, perhaps the statement is unknown because of the contradiction, but I\'m not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I\'m misapplying the rules.Let me ignore each rule again.\n\nFact 12: If something is unkind and black, then it is red. So unkind ∧ black → red.\n\nBut Bob is unkind (from being old) and black, so he must be red. But he\'s not red; he\'s black. So that\'s a confirmation. Therefore, our assumption that Bob is old must be right. But how?\n\nWait, perhaps the mistake is in assuming that Bob is flat. Let me ignore that again.\n\nFact 11: Black things are flat. So Bob is black → flat.\n\nFact 14: All flat things are old. So Bob is flat → old.\n\nSo Bob must be old. But then, as per fact 12, he must be red, which confirms his color being black.\n\nTherefore, the only way to resolve this is that Bob can be both black and unkind, but since he\'s old, he must be unkind. So this creates a confirmation, meaning that the initial premises are consistent. Therefore, the statement "Bob is young" might be false because the only way to avoid the confirmation is if Bob is young, but according to the facts, he must be old.\n\nWait, this is getting simple. Let me try another approach.\n\nLet\'s list all the attributes for each person:\n\n- Bob: loud, black\n- Dave: unkind, blue, old\n- Fiona: blue, flat\n- Gary: loud, blue, flat\n\nFrom fact 14, all flat things are old. So Fiona is flat → old. Gary is flat → old.\n\nFrom fact 13, all old things are unkind. So Fiona and Gary are old → unkind.\n\nFrom fact 15, all loud, unkind things are black. Bob is loud and, if he\'s unkind, then he must be black, which he is. But does Bob have to be unkind? From fact 13, if he\'s old, he\'s unkind. But from fact 14, he\'s flat → old. So Bob is old → unkind.\n\nSo Bob is unkind and black → from fact 12, he must be red. But he\'s black, not red. Confirmation.\n\nTherefore, the only way to resolve this is that Bob is young, which would mean he\'s not flat (from fact 14), but he is black, so from fact 11, he must be flat. Therefore, he must be old, which leads to the confirmation.\n\nThis suggests that the premises are consistent, meaning that the statement "Bob is young" is actually false because otherwise, we have a confirmation. Alternatively, perhaps the answer is known because of the consistency.\n\nWait, but in logic, if the premises lead to a confirmation, it means that the premises are cons