ald eagle doesn't see the dog, so it doesn't need the mouse. Therefore, statement 8 doesn't apply, so the mouse can see the bald eagle.\n\nBut from statement 10, the bald eagle is rough, so it sees the mouse. So, the bald eagle sees the mouse, which would make the statement true.\n\nWait, but if the bald eagle sees the mouse, then from statement 11, the mouse is kind. So, the mouse is kind, which from statement 14, makes the mouse rough. So, the mouse is rough, which from statement 10, means someone else (like the bald eagle) sees the mouse. So, it's consistent.\n\nTherefore, the statement is true.\n\nBut wait, I'm getting conflicting conclusions. Let me try to outline the chain again.\n\n1. Bald eagle is kind (statement 1) → rough (statement 14) → sees mouse (statement 10). So, bald eagle sees mouse.\n\n2. If the bald eagle sees the mouse, then the mouse is kind (statement 11) → mouse is rough (statement 14).\n\n3. Mouse is rough → someone else (like the bald eagle) sees the mouse (statement 10). So, consistent.\n\n4. Now, does the bald eagle need the mouse? From statement 13: If someone sees the dog, they need the mouse. So, if the bald eagle sees the dog, it needs the mouse. But does the bald eagle see the dog? The bald eagle likes the dog, but that doesn't imply seeing. So, perhaps the bald eagle doesn't see the dog, so it doesn't need the mouse. Therefore, statement 8 doesn't apply, so the mouse can see the bald eagle.\n\nBut from statement 10, the bald eagle sees the mouse, so the statement is true.\n\nWait, but statement 8 says if someone needs the mouse, then the mouse doesn't see the bald eagle. So, if the bald eagle needs the mouse, then the mouse doesn't see the bald eagle. But if the bald eagle doesn't need the mouse, then the mouse can see the bald eagle.\n\nSo, if the bald eagle doesn't need the mouse, then the mouse can see the bald eagle. But from statement 10, the bald eagle sees the mouse, so the statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer 