      "text": "Wait, no, that's conflicting. Let me re-examine statement 17: If someone is nice and they chase the rabbit, then the rabbit is nice. So the subject is the chaser. So if the chaser is nice and chases the rabbit, then the rabbit is nice.\n\nIn our case, the lion is nice (statement 3) and the lion sees the rabbit (statement 6). But does the lion chase the rabbit? Statement 6 says the lion sees the rabbit, but statement 11 says the tiger chases the rabbit. So the lion doesn't chase the rabbit. Therefore, statement 17 doesn't apply because the lion doesn't chase the rabbit.\n\nWait, so the only chaser of the rabbit is the tiger (statement 11). The tiger is nice (statement 12). So the tiger is nice and chases the rabbit. Therefore, according to statement 17, the rabbit is nice.\n\nWait, that's a different conclusion. So if the tiger is nice and chases the rabbit, then the rabbit is nice. So the rabbit is nice.\n\nBut earlier, I thought the rabbit is mean because the rabbit is mean and ignores the tiger. But that's a different rule. Let me check statement 17 again.\n\nStatement 17: If someone is nice and they chase the rabbit, then the rabbit is nice.\n\nSo the subject is the chaser. The tiger is nice (statement 12) and chases the rabbit (statement 11). Therefore, the rabbit is nice.\n\nSo that would make the rabbit nice, which would make the statement \"The rabbit is not nice\" false.\n\nBut wait, is there any other rule that could affect this? Let me check statement 15: If the rabbit eats the tiger, then the tiger is nice. But that doesn't directly affect the rabbit's niceness.\n\nStatement 17 is the key here. Since the tiger is nice and chases the rabbit, the rabbit must be nice. Therefore, the statement \"The rabbit is not nice\" is false.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought the rabbit is mean because the rabbit is mean and ignores the tiger. But that's a different rule. Let me check statement 17 again.\n\nWait, no, statement 17 is about the chaser being nice