agle and sees it, they are big. The rabbit eats the bald eagle, so it's likely that it sees it, making the rabbit big. But statement 14 says if someone eats the bald eagle, they are warm. So, the rabbit is both big and warm.\n\nFrom statement 15: If someone needs the rabbit and is big, then the rabbit ignores the mouse. But we don't know if anyone needs the rabbit, so this might not be directly relevant.\n\nFrom statement 17: If someone is cold and big, then they don't need the bald eagle. But the rabbit is warm, so this doesn't apply.\n\nSo, the rabbit is big and warm, doesn't need the lion, so the statement is false.\n\nWait, but the question is about the rabbit needing the lion, not the other way around. So, the rabbit doesn't need the lion because it's warm, so the statement is false.\n\nBut I'm getting conflicting conclusions. Let me try to outline the steps clearly:\n\n1. Rabbit eats the bald eagle (statement 10).\n2. Therefore, rabbit is warm (statement 14).\n3. Therefore, rabbit doesn't need the lion (statement 16).\n4. Therefore, the statement \"The rabbit needs the lion\" is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B) False",
