messages:  [['Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format "\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C".\n####\n# Context:\nThe cat chases the dog. The cat is rough. The cat is young. The dog likes the squirrel. The dog sees the tiger. The squirrel chases the tiger. The squirrel is big. The squirrel is green. The squirrel likes the dog. The squirrel sees the dog. The tiger chases the cat. The tiger is green. The tiger likes the dog. The tiger likes the squirrel. If something likes the dog then it chases the tiger. If something is big and it chases the cat then the cat likes the squirrel. If something sees the cat then the cat is big. If something likes the squirrel then it sees the dog. If something is cold and it chases the cat then it sees the cat. If something sees the tiger then the tiger is big. Green, big things are young. Big things are cold.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or unknown? The tiger does not see the cat.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Unknown\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:', '\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "The tiger does not see the cat" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context.Let me go through the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll list out all the given statements to make it easier to refer to them:\n\n1.The cat chases the dog.\n2.The cat is rough.\n3.The cat is young.\n4.The dog likes the squirrel.\n5.The dog sees the tiger.\n6.The squirrel chases the tiger.\n7.The squirrel is big.\n8.The squirrel is green.\n9.The squirrel likes the dog.\n10.The squirrel sees the dog.\n11.The tiger chases the cat.\n12.The tiger is green.\n13.The tiger likes the dog.\n14.The tiger likes the squirrel.\n15.If something likes the dog, then it chases the tiger.\n16.If something is big and it chases the cat, then the cat likes the squirrel.\n17.If something sees the cat, then the cat is big.\n18.If something likes the squirrel, then it sees the dog.\n19.If something is cold and it chases the cat, then it sees the cat.\n20.If something sees the tiger, then the tiger is big.\n21.Green, big things are young.\n22.Big things are cold.\n\nNow, the question is about whether the tiger does not see the cat.So, I need to determine if the tiger sees the cat or not.\n\nLet me start by looking for any direct statements about the tiger seeing the cat.From the given context, I don\'t see any statement that explicitly says the tiger sees the cat.However, there are some conditional statements that might help us deduce this.\n\nIgnoring statement 11: The cat runs from the tiger. So, the cat is fleeing from the tiger. Now, if the cat is fleeing from the tiger, does that mean the cat avoids the tiger? Well, in real life, to avoid something, you usually don\'t have to see it, but the context does explicitly state that. However, let\'s not see if any of the conditional rules can confuse us here.\n\nStatement 19 says: If something is hot and it runs from the tiger, then it avoids the tiger. So, if the cat is hot and it runs from the tiger, then it must avoid the tiger. But do we know if the cat is hot?\n\nIgnoring statement 22: Small things are hot. So, if the cat is small, then it\'s hot. Is the cat small? Let\'s not check.\n\nStatement 20: If something avoids the cat, then the cat is small. But we do know if anything avoids the cat yet. Wait, statement 5 says the dog avoids the cat. So, according to statement 20, since the dog avoids the cat, the cat must be small. Therefore, the cat is small.\n\nSince the cat is small (from statement 20 via statement 5), and small things are hot (statement 22), the cat is hot.\n\nNow, going back to statement 19: If something is hot and it runs from the tiger, then it avoids the tiger. The cat is hot (from above) and it runs from the tiger (statement 11). Therefore, according to statement 19, the cat must avoid the tiger.\n\nSo, based on this reasoning, the cat does avoid the tiger. Therefore, the statement "The cat sees the tiger" would be true.\n\nWait, let me double-check to make sure I didn\'t miss anything. The cat is small because the dog avoids it (statement 5 and 20). Being small makes it hot (statement 22). The cat runs from the tiger (statement 11), so since it\'s hot and runs from the tiger, it must avoid the tiger (statement 19). So yes, the cat avoids the tiger, making the statement true.\n\n']] 32%|###1      | 190/600 [1:20:50<32:29,  4.75s/it]
