e is. But that doesn't necessarily mean he is red.\n\nWait, but statement 17 says if Bob is furry and red, then he's flat. Bob is furry and flat, so if he's red, that's consistent. But does that mean he is red?\n\nAlternatively, maybe Bob is not red. Let's see.\n\nIf Bob is not red, then he's green (from 16 and 12). So he's green, but not red. That would mean he's not a red, green thing, so statement 15 doesn't apply to him. So that's okay.\n\nBut then, statement 18 says red things are big. Bob is big, but if he's not red, that's okay.\n\nSo, is there any statement that says Bob is red?\n\nLooking back, statement 12: If something is flat and warm, then it's green. So Bob is green.\n\nStatement 16: If something is flat, then it's green. So Bob is green.\n\nNow, statement 15: All red, green things are furry. So if Bob is red and green, he must be furry, which he is. But that doesn't mean he is red.\n\nSo, is there any other statement that would make Bob red?\n\nStatement 17: If Bob is furry and red, then he's flat. But Bob is furry and flat, so if he's red, that's consistent. But again, that doesn't necessarily mean he is red.\n\nWait, but statement 12 says if something is flat and warm, then it's green. So Bob is green.\n\nStatement 16 says if something is flat, then it's green. So Bob is green.\n\nNow, statement 15 says all red, green things are furry. So if Bob is red and green, he must be furry, which he is. But that doesn't mean he is red.\n\nSo, is there any other way to determine if Bob is red?\n\nLooking at statement 18: Red things are big. Bob is big, but that doesn't mean he's red.\n\nStatement 19: All blue things are furry. Bob is blue and furry, so that's consistent.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Bob is green, blue, big, furry, flat, and cold.\n\nBut is he red? The statements don't explicitly say Bob is red. They only say that if something is flat and warm, it's green, and if something is flat, it's green. They also say that all red, green things are furry, but that doesn't mean Bob is red.\n\nTherefore, based on the given information, we cannot definitively say that Bob is red. It's possible, but not certain.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Statement 12 says if something is flat and warm, then it's green. Bob is flat and warm, so he's green. So he's green.\n\nStatement 16 says if something is flat, then it's green. So he's green.\n\nNow, statement 15 says all red, green things are furry. So if Bob is red and green, he must be furry, which he is. But that doesn't mean he is red.\n\nSo, is there any other statement that would make Bob red?\n\nStatement 17: If Bob is furry and red, then he's flat. Bob is furry and flat, so if he's red, that's consistent. But again, that doesn't mean he is red.\n\nSo, in conclusion, based on the given information, we cannot definitively determine whether Bob is red or not. Therefore, the statement \"Bob is red\" is unknown.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C) Unknown",
