ees the bear, then the bear chases the cow.So, if someone sees the bear, the bear chases the cow.But we know from statement 4 that the bear chases the tiger, not the cow.So, does that mean that the bear doesn\'t see the person?Or perhaps the person doesn\'t see the bear?Wait, that might be a bit confusing.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s consider statement 12: If the tiger is round and kind, then the tiger chases the bear.We know the cow is round (statement 6), but the tiger\'s roundness isn\'t directly stated.However, the bald eagle is round (statement 3), and the cow is round.The tiger\'s characteristics aren\'t given except that it sees the cow (statement 7).So, we don\'t know if the tiger is round or kind.Therefore, statement 12 might not apply because we don\'t know if the tiger is round and kind.\n\nLooking back at statement 11: If someone chases the tiger, then the tiger eats the bear.So, if someone is chasing the tiger, the tiger will eat the bear.But who is chasing the tiger?From statement 4, the bear chases the tiger.So, the bear is chasing the tiger, which would mean, according to statement 11, that the tiger eats the bear.But that seems contradictory because if the bear is chasing the tiger, and the tiger eats the bear, that would imply some sort of conflict.But perhaps that\'s a possible scenario.\n\nWait, but statement 10 says if someone chases the tiger, they are rough.So, the bear is chasing the tiger, so the bear is rough.That\'s an interesting point.So, the bear is rough because it\'s chasing the tiger.\n\nNow, going back to the original question: Does the bear see the bald eagle?Let\'s see if any statements link the bear\'s perception of the bald eagle.\n\nStatement 8: If someone is young and sees the bald eagle, they see the bear.So, seeing the bald eagle implies seeing the bear, but only if the person is young.But does that mean the bear sees the bald eagle?Not necessarily.It\'s about someone else\'s perspective.\n\nStatement 13: If someone is young, they see the bald eagle.So, young people see the bald eagle, which from statement 8, would mean they also see the bear.But again, this is about someone else\'s perspective, not the bear\'s.\n\nIs there any statement that says if the bear sees someone, then something happens?Let\'s check statement 15: If someone sees the bear, then the bear chases the cow.But we know from statement 4 that the bear chases the tiger, not the cow.So, if someone sees the bear, the bear would chase the cow, but the bear is already chasing the tiger.So, perhaps the bear doesn\'t see that someone is watching, or maybe the person doesn\'t see the bear.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bear doesn\'t see the bald eagle because the statements don\'t directly link them.The bald eagle is chasing the cow, and the bear is chasing the tiger.The tiger sees the cow, but there\'s no direct link between the bear and the bald eagle.\n\nWait, but statement 14 says if someone chases the cow, they chase the bear.The bald eagle chases the cow, so according to statement 14, the bald eagle chases the bear.So, the bald eagle is chasing the bear.Now, if the bald eagle is chasing the bear, does that mean the bear sees the bald eagle?Because if the bald eagle is chasing the bear, the bear would likely see the bald eagle, right?Because you can\'t chase someone without them being aware, or at least seeing you.\n\nSo, if the bald eagle is chasing the bear, then the bear must see the bald eagle.Therefore, the statement "The bear sees the bald eagle" would be true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check.The bald eagle chases the cow (statement 1).From statement 14, if someone chases the cow, they chase the bear.So, the bald eagle chases the cow, so the bald eagle chases the bear.Therefore, the bald eagle is chasing the bear.Now, if the bald eagle is chasing the bear, does that mean the bear sees the bald eagle?It\'s a bit of a logical leap, but in real life, if something is chasing you, you would see it.So, perhaps the bear does see the bald eagle.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bear doesn\'t see the bald eagle because the statements don\'t explicitly say so.But given that the bald eagle is chasing the bear, it\'s reasonable to infer that the bear would see the bald eagle.\n\nWait, but let me think again.The statements are all conditional, so maybe we can\'t make that assumption.The fact that the bald eagle chases the bear doesn\'t necessarily mean the bear sees the bald eagle.It could be that the bald eagle is chasing the bear, but the bear doesn\'t see the bald eagle for some reason.However, in most logical reasoning, if A chases B, it\'s implied that B is aware of A, at least to some extent.\n\nBut perhaps the problem is designed to not make that assumption.So, maybe we can\'t conclude that the bear sees the bald eagle just because the bald eagle is chasing the bear.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s look at statement 15 again: If someone sees the bear, then the bear chases the cow.But we know the bear is chasing the tiger, not the cow.So, if someone sees the bear, the bear would have to chase the cow, but it\'s already chasing the tiger.Therefore, perhaps no one sees the bear, because if they did, the bear would have to switch from chasing the tiger to chasing the cow, which contradicts statement 4.\n\nWait, that\'s an interesting point.If someone sees the bear, then the bear chases the cow (statement 15).But the bear is already chasing the tiger (statement 4).So, if someone sees the bear, the bear would have to chase the cow, which contradicts the fact that it\'s chasing the tiger.Therefore, perhaps no one sees the bear, because if they did, the bear\'s action would change, which it can\'t because it\'s already chasing the tiger.\n\nSo, if everyone sees the bear, then the bear sees the bald eagle because the statements link them directly. Therefore, the statement "The bear sees the bald eagle" would be known because there\'s information that directly connects the bear\'s perception of the bald eagl