 And since the tiger avoids the cat, it also avoids the cow (statement 16). So, the tiger avoids the cow.\n\nNow, statement 11: If the tiger avoids the cow, then the tiger dislikes the cat. So, the tiger dislikes the cat.\n\nSo, the tiger dislikes the cat, but statement 7 says the tiger likes the cow. So, the tiger likes the cow but dislikes the cat.\n\nIs there any contradiction here? Not necessarily. The tiger can like the cow and dislike the cat.\n\nBut wait, statement 7 says the tiger likes the cow, but through deductions, we found that the tiger avoids the cow because it's warm. So, does avoiding the cow mean the tiger dislikes the cow?\n\nWait, statement 13 says if something avoids the cow, then the cow dislikes the tiger. But it doesn't say that the tiger dislikes the cow. It only says the cow dislikes the tiger.\n\nSo, the tiger avoiding the cow doesn't necessarily mean the tiger dislikes the cow. It just means the cow dislikes the tiger.\n\nSo, the tiger can avoid the cow because it's warm (statement 16), but that doesn't mean the tiger dislikes the cow. The tiger still likes the cow (statement 7), but because it's warm, it avoids the cow.\n\nSo, the tiger likes the cow but avoids the cow because it's warm. That's possible.\n\nSo, the statement \"The tiger likes the cow\" is true because statement 7 says so, and our deductions don't contradict that. The tiger's avoidance of the cow is due to being warm, not because it dislikes the cow.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) True.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A",
