, the squirrel does see the rabbit, making the statement \"The squirrel does not see the rabbit\" false.\n\nBut earlier, I thought that avoiding implies ignoring, but chasing requires seeing. So, the squirrel must see the rabbit to chase it. Therefore, the statement is false.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any condition that says if someone chases something, they see it. I don't see any explicit statement, but logically, chasing implies seeing. So, the squirrel must see the rabbit to chase it.\n\nTherefore, the squirrel does see the rabbit, making the statement \"The squirrel does not see the rabbit\" false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe squirrel chases the rabbit, which implies it must see the rabbit. Therefore, the statement is false.\n\nThe correct option is: B",
