om point 18, all gentle, small people are foolish. Since Anne is gentle and small, she must be foolish. Wait, that\'s conflicting with the statement we\'re evaluating, which says Anne is smart. So according to this, Anne is foolish, making the statement "Anne is smart" true.\n\nBut let me double-check to make sure I didn\'t miss anything. Let\'s go through each step again.\n\nAnne is square (point 1). Square people are green (point 15), so Anne is green. Green people are small (point 14), so Anne is small. Small people are cruel (point 13), so Anne is cruel. Cruel people are gentle (point 17), so Anne is gentle. Now, gentle and small people are foolish (point 18), so Anne is foolish. Therefore, the statement that Anne is smart is true.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any other rule that might affect this. For example, point 19 says square, foolish people are green. Since Anne is square and we\'ve deduced she\'s foolish, she must be green, which aligns with our earlier conclusion. There\'s also point 20 about Fiona, but that doesn\'t affect Anne directly.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Anne is foolish, so the statement "Anne is smart" is true.\n']]
