isits the mouse and the mouse needs the squirrel, then it needs the rabbit.So, the subject is "something" that visits the mouse.So, who visits the mouse?From statement 7, the squirrel chases the mouse, but does that mean the squirrel visits the mouse?Not necessarily.Visiting and chasing are different actions.So, perhaps the squirrel doesn\'t visit the mouse, but chases it.\n\nWait, but from statement 17, since the mouse needs the squirrel, the squirrel visits the mouse.So, the squirrel visits the mouse.So, the squirrel is the "something" that visits the mouse.Therefore, according to statement 14, since the squirrel visits the mouse and the mouse needs the squirrel, then the squirrel needs the rabbit.\n\nSo, the squirrel needs the rabbit.Now, looking at statement 12: If the squirrel is young and needs the rabbit, then it visits the rabbit.The squirrel is young (statement 11) and now we know it needs the rabbit, so the squirrel visits the rabbit.\n\nNow, statement 16: If something visits the bear and the bear visits the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the rabbit.We know the squirrel visits the rabbit, but does the bear visit the squirrel?Let\'s see.\n\nFrom statement 18: If the mouse visits the bear and the mouse visits the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the bear.The mouse visits the bear (statement 2 says the mouse chases the bear, but does that mean it visits?Not necessarily.Visiting and chasing are different.So, unless there\'s a statement that says the mouse visits the bear, we can\'t assume that.Wait, statement 2 is the mouse chases the bear, but statement 4 is the mouse visits the rabbit.So, the mouse doesn\'t necessarily visit the bear unless stated.\n\nWait, statement 18 says if the mouse visits the bear and the mouse visits the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the bear.But we don\'t know if the mouse visits the bear.The mouse chases the bear, but that\'s different from visiting.So, unless the mouse visits the bear, we can\'t trigger statement 18.\n\n