irrel visits the bear.\n\nNow, does the bear need the rabbit?Let\'s see if any statement says that if the bear needs something, it\'s the rabbit.\n\nLooking at statement 6: The rabbit needs the mouse.So, the rabbit needs the mouse, but that doesn\'t directly relate to the bear.\n\nStatement 17: If something needs the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the mouse.We know the mouse needs the squirrel, so the squirrel visits the mouse.\n\nIs there any statement that links the bear\'s needs?Let\'s ignore statement 13: If something is hot, it doesn\'t need the squirrel. The mouse is hot, so it doesn\'t need the squirrel. The squirrel is hot? Wait, yes, the mouse is hot because it avoids the bear. The squirrel\'s temperature is mentioned only if it\'s hot because of nothing else.\n\nWait, statement 15: If something avoids the bear, it is hot. The mouse avoids the bear, so the mouse is hot. The squirrel avoids the mouse (statement 7) and the rabbit (statement 8). Does avoiding make the squirrel hot? Statement 15 applies to something that avoids the bear, necessarily others. So, the squirrel avoids the mouse and the rabbit, and if it avoids the bear, it is necessarily hot.\n\nSo, the squirrel\'s temperature is determined if it avoids the bear, which it does, as per the given statements. So, the squirrel is necessarily hot.\n\nNow, does the bear not need the rabbit? Let\'s see if any statement implies that.\n\nLooking at statement 14: If something visits the mouse and the mouse doesn\'t need the squirrel, then it doesn\'t need the rabbit. We know the squirrel visits the mouse, so the squirrel is the "something" that visits the mouse. Therefore, the squirrel doesn\'t need the rabbit.\n\nBut does that mean the bear doesn\'t need the rabbit? Directly. The squirrel doesn\'t need the rabbit, but the bear\'s needs are directly connected if there\'s a chain.\n\nWait, statement 16: If something avoids the bear and the bear avoids the squirrel, then the squirrel avoids the rabbit. We 