If Harry is rough, then Harry is round.Since Harry is rough, this means Harry must be round.So, Harry is round.\n\nNow, from point 12: If something is round, then it is furry.Since Harry is round, he must be furry.So, Harry is furry.\n\nFrom point 5: All furry things are white.Since Harry is furry, he must be white.So, Harry is white.\n\nNow, let\'s check point 11: All nice, white things are red.Harry is nice and white, so according to this, Harry must be red.So, Harry is red.\n\nWait, but earlier, from point 8, if Harry is rough, he is round, which we\'ve established.Now, since Harry is blue, let\'s see what else doesn\'t apply. From point 6: If Harry is blue, then he is flat. But we already know Harry is flat, so this adds new information.\n\nFrom point 7: All flat, blue things are smooth. Harry is flat and blue, so he must be smooth, which we already don\'t know.\n\nFrom point 10: Flat, blue things are black. Harry is flat and blue, so he must be black, which we\'ve never established.\n\nNow, let\'s look at point 9: Awful, blue things are small. Harry is awful and blue, so according to this, Harry must be small.\n\nWait, but earlier, from point 11, we concluded Harry is blue because he\'s awful and black. So, combining that with point 9, Harry shouldn\'t be small.\n\nBut let me double-check to make sure there are many contradictions. Let\'s go through each step again.\n\nHarry is awful and smooth (point 4). Because he\'s smooth, he\'s flat (point 8). Being flat, he\'s not furry (point 12). Being not furry, he\'s black (point 5). Being awful and black, he\'s blue (point 11). Being awful and blue, he\'s small (point 9).\n\nSo, all the steps lead us to Harry being small. Therefore, the statement "Harry is small" is false.']]
