ion (statement 2), so if the bear is blue, then the lion is nice.But we don\'t know if the bear is blue.\n\nAlternatively, statement 17: If the bear is not green, then the bear likes the lion.But the bear already likes the lion (statement 2), so this doesn\'t add new info.\n\nStatement 18: If something visits the bear, then the bear likes the lion.Again, the bear already likes the lion, so not much help.\n\nWait, the mouse visits the squirrel (statement 9).The squirrel is nice (statement 11), so from statement 20, the squirrel is blue.So, squirrel is blue.\n\nNow, the mouse eats the squirrel (statement 6).So, from statement 12: If something eats the squirrel, then the squirrel eats the mouse.So, since the mouse eats the squirrel, the squirrel must eat the mouse.But that seems a bit circular, but it\'s a given.\n\nNow, the mouse likes the bear (statement 7) and the mouse likes the lion (statement 8).So, the mouse likes both the bear and the lion.\n\nFrom statement 16: If something eats the mouse, then it likes the lion.But the squirrel eats the mouse (from statement 12, because the mouse eats the squirrel, so the squirrel eats the mouse).So, the squirrel eats the mouse, which means the squirrel must like the lion (from statement 16).But the squirrel is nice (statement 11), so from statement 20, it\'s blue.\n\nWait, but the squirrel is blue, and from statement 19: If something likes the lion and the lion is blue, then the lion likes the mouse.The squirrel likes the lion (from statement 16, because the squirrel eats the mouse, so it must like the lion).So, the squirrel likes the lion, and if the lion is blue, then the lion likes the mouse.\n\nBut we don\'t know if the lion is blue yet.Let\'s see if we can determine the lion\'s color.\n\nFrom statement 15: If something likes the lion and it is blue, then the lion is nice.The bear likes the lion (statement 2), so if the bear is blue, then the lion is nice.But the lion is already nice because the squirrel is nice and the squirrel is blue, but that doesn\'t directly affect the lion\'s niceness.\n\nWait, the lion is big (statement 3), but that\'s unrelated.The lion visits the mouse (statement 4) and the squirrel (statement 5).So, the lion visits both the mouse and the squirrel.\n\nFrom statement 13: If something visits the bear and it is green, then it is nice.The lion doesn\'t visit the bear, as per the given statements.The lion visits the mouse and the squirrel, but not the bear.So, statement 13 doesn\'t apply to the lion.\n\nSo, the lion\'s color isn\'t directly determined, but let\'s see if we can infer it.\n\nFrom statement 19: If nothing dislikes the lion and the lion is red, then the lion dislikes the mouse. The mouse dislikes the lion (statement 8), so if the lion is red, then the lion dislikes the mouse. But the lion does not visit the mouse (statement 4), which might imply no disliking, but it\'s explicitly stated.\n\nAlternatively, the lion is small, but that\'s all we know about the lion\'s attributes.\n\nWait, the squirrel is red (from statement 20, since it\'s mean). The mouse avoids the squirrel (statement 6), so from statement 12, the squirrel avoids the mouse. So, the squirrel avoids the mouse, which means the squirrel must dislike the lion (statement 16). So, the squirrel dislikes the lion.\n\nNow, the squirrel is red and dislikes the lion. From statement 19: If nothing dislikes the lion and the lion is red, then the lion dislikes the mouse. So, if the lion is red, then the lion dislikes the mouse. But we do know if the lion is red yet.\n\nWait, the lion is small, but that\'s it. Let\'s see if the lion\'s color can be determined.\n\nFrom statement 15: If nothing dislikes the lion and it is red, then the lion is mean. The bear dislikes the lion (statement 2), so if the bear is red, then the lion is mean. But the lion is already mean because the squirrel is mean, but that does necessarily mean the lion is mean. Wait, yes, the squirrel is mean, but the lion\'s meanness 