ould be false.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought the squirrel avoids itself. So, which is correct?\n\nLet me re-examine the chain:\n\n1. Lion visits cat (statement 11) → Lion likes cat (statement 14).\n2. Lion likes cat → Lion chases squirrel (statement 17).\n3. Lion chases squirrel → Squirrel visits cat (statement 20).\n4. Squirrel visits cat → Squirrel likes cat (statement 14).\n5. Squirrel likes cat → Squirrel chases squirrel (statement 17).\n\nSo, according to this, the squirrel chases itself. Therefore, the statement "The squirrel does not chase the squirrel" is false.\n\nBut wait, in the initial reasoning, I thought the squirrel avoids itself because of the chain involving the lion avoiding the squirrel. But that seems conflicting.\n\nLet me check that chain again:\n\n1. Lion ignores cat (statement 11) → Lion dislikes cat (statement 14).\n2. Lion dislikes cat → Lion avoids squirrel (statement 17).\n3. Lion avoids squirrel → Squirrel ignores cat (statement 20).\n4. Squirrel ignores cat → Squirrel dislikes cat (statement 14).\n5. Squirrel dislikes cat → Squirrel avoids squirrel (statement 17).\n\nSo, in this chain, the squirrel avoids itself.\n\nBut in the other chain, the squirrel chases itself.\n\nThis is a contradiction. So, which chain is correct?\n\nI think the confusion arises because the same statements can lead to two different conclusions depending on the order of application.\n\nBut in logic, if two different conclusions can be drawn, that would mean the system is inconsistent, which is a problem. However, in this case, perhaps one chain is more direct than the other.\n\nLet me see which chain is more directly supported by the given statements.\n\nThe first chain:\n\n- Lion visits cat → Lion likes cat (statement 14).\n- Lion likes cat → Lion chases squirrel (statement 17).\n- Lion chases squirrel → Squirrel visits cat (statement 20).\n- Squirrel visits cat → Squirrel likes cat (statement 14).\n- Squirrel likes cat → Squirrel chases squirrel (statement 17)