s mean, but that was based on a misapplication of the rules. Let me correct that.\n\nSo, the correct reasoning is:\n\n1. Gary is young → smart (statement 11).\n2. Gary is smart and young → nice (statement 17).\n3. Gary is nice and smart → round (statement 18).\n4. Gary is cold and round → kind (statement 12).\n5. Gary is kind and cold → rough (statement 13).\n\nSo, all these connect without contradiction. Therefore, Gary is nice.\n\nWait, but in the initial reasoning, I thought he was mean, but that was incorrect because I misapplied the rules. So, the correct conclusion is that Gary is nice.\n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s any rule that says being rough affects niceness. From statement 2, Charlie is rough and nice. So, being rough doesn\'t necessarily mean not nice. So, Gary being rough doesn\'t conflict with him being nice.\n\nTherefore, the statement "Gary is nice" is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A', '']
