final_output:  ['Wait, but the question is whether the tiger does not visit the bald eagle. So, if the tiger ignores the bald eagle, does that mean it doesn\'t visit? Or is visiting a different action?\n\nIn the context, visiting seems to be a positive action, while ignoring is not visiting. So, if the tiger ignores the bald eagle, it doesn\'t visit it.\n\nTherefore, the statement "The tiger does not visit the bald eagle" is true.\n\nWait, but let me check again. The squirrel visits the tiger (statement 10). The squirrel chases the tiger (statement 9). The squirrel chases the bald eagle (statement 8). The squirrel doesn\'t visit the lion because the condition in statement 13 isn\'t met.\n\nThe lion needs the tiger (statement 7). The lion is cold and kind (statements 5 and 6). The lion doesn\'t need the bald eagle because the condition in statement 14 isn\'t met.\n\nThe bald eagle is cold, rough, needs the tiger, visits the squirrel, and is chased by the squirrel (statements 1-4, 8). The bald eagle chases the squirrel only if someone visits it (statement 15). But does someone visit the bald eagle? The squirrel doesn\'t visit the bald eagle, but the tiger chases the bald eagle (from statement 20, because the squirrel chases the tiger, so the tiger chases the bald eagle). So, the tiger chases the bald eagle, but does the tiger visit the bald eagle?\n\nFrom statement 19, the tiger ignores the bald eagle because it flees the deer. So, the tiger doesn\'t visit the bald eagle.\n\nTherefore, the statement "The tiger does not visit the bald eagle" is true.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. The tiger chases the bald eagle (from statement 20), but does that mean the tiger visits the bald eagle? Or is chasing a different action?\n\nIn the context, visiting and chasing are separate actions. For example, the squirrel visits the tiger (statement 10) and chases the tiger (statement 9). So, visiting and chasing are different. Therefore, the tiger chases the bald eagle but doesn\'t visi