mething chases the squirrel, then it eats the cat (statement 9).\n- If the mouse eats the cat, then the cat chases the squirrel (statement 10).\n- If something eats the mouse, then the mouse chases the squirrel (statement 11).\n- If something chases the squirrel, then the squirrel eats the cat (statement 12).\n\nNone of these directly link the squirrel to the bear. The bear is linked to the cat, and the cat is linked to the mouse and the squirrel. The mouse is linked to the squirrel.\n\nSo, the squirrel\'s actions towards the bear aren\'t directly addressed in any of the statements. Therefore, based on the given information, we don\'t have any statements that indicate whether the squirrel chases the bear or not. All the statements are about the squirrel being chased or chasing others, but not about chasing the bear.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is that the statement is unknown because there\'s no information provided about the squirrel\'s actions towards the bear.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C) Unknown\n\nThe squirrel\'s relationship with the bear isn\'t addressed in the given statements. While the squirrel interacts with the cat and the mouse, there\'s no information indicating whether the squirrel chases the bear. Therefore, the statement is unknown.', '']
