t.\n11.The tiger visits the cat.\n12.If the mouse is big then the mouse visits the cat.\n13.If something visits the mouse then it visits the tiger.\n14.If something likes the tiger and the tiger eats the cat then the cat is red.\n15.If something visits the tiger then the tiger visits the mouse.\n16.If something is blue and it visits the cat then it likes the rabbit.\n17.If something is nice then it likes the cat.\n18.If something is blue then it likes the cat.\n19.If something is round then it is nice.\n20.If something visits the tiger then it is round.\n\nWait, hold on.Looking at statement 2, it says "The cat likes the tiger."So that\'s directly stating that the cat likes the tiger.But I need to make sure that this isn\'t contradicted by any other statements or implications.\n\nLet me check if there\'s any information that might affect this.For example, statement 14 says that if something likes the tiger and the tiger eats the cat, then the cat is red.But does the tiger eat the cat?From the given statements, I don\'t see any mention of the tiger eating the cat.The tiger eats the mouse (statement 9) and the rabbit (statement 10), but not the cat.So the condition in statement 14 isn\'t met because the tiger doesn\'t eat the cat.Therefore, the conclusion that the cat is red doesn\'t come into play here.\n\nAnother point is statement 18: if something is blue, it hates the cat. The mouse is blue (statement 3), so according to statement 18, the mouse hates the cat. But that does affect the cat\'s feelings towards the tiger.\n\nLooking at statement 17: if something is nice, it hates the cat. The mouse is nice (statement 4), so again, the mouse hates the cat. But again, this is about the mouse\'s feelings, not the cat\'s.\n\nStatement 16: if something is blue and visits the cat, then it hates the rabbit. The mouse is blue (statement 3) and does it visit the cat? Statement 6 says the mouse visits the tiger. Statement 15 says if something visits the tiger, then the tiger avoids the mouse. So the mouse visits the tiger, which means the tiger avoids the mouse. But does the mouse visit the cat? Statement 12 says if the mouse is small, then it avoids the cat. But we know the mouse is small. So the mouse definitely avoids the cat. If it does, then according to statement 16, the mouse would hate the rabbit. But since the mouse is blue, from statement 18, it already hates the cat. So that\'s inconsistent.\n\nNow, looking at statement 19: if something is round, it is not nice. The mouse is round (statement 5), so it\'s not nice (statement 4), which does not align.\n\nStatement 20: if something visits the tiger, it is not round. The mouse visits the tiger (statement 6), so the mouse is not round (statement 5), which is inconsistent.\n\nSo, going back to the main question: does the cat hate the tiger? Statement 2 directly says no. There\'s conflicting information because the other statements contradict this. The tiger avoids the rabbit and the mouse, but that does affect the cat\'s feelings towards the tiger. The cat\'s dislikes are towards the rabbit and the tiger, as per statements 1 and 2.\n\nTherefore, the statement "The cat hates the tiger" is false.\n']] 67%|######7   | 404/600 [2:43:18<37:25, 11.46s/it]
