atement 19 says if something eats the mouse, then it is blue.So, the squirrel eats the mouse, so the squirrel is blue.\n\nWait, that\'s conflicting with the earlier conclusion that the squirrel is orange.\n\nWait, I think I made a mistake earlier.\n\nLet me correct that.\n\nFrom statement 19: If something eats the mouse, then it is blue.So, the squirrel eats the mouse, so the squirrel is blue.\n\nTherefore, the squirrel is blue.\n\nWait, that\'s different from what I thought earlier.\n\nSo, let\'s retrace.\n\nFrom statement 19: If something eats the mouse, then it is blue.So, the squirrel eats the mouse, so the squirrel is blue.\n\nTherefore, the squirrel is blue.\n\nSo, the statement "The squirrel is not blue" would be false.\n\nWait, but that\'s conflicting with the earlier conclusion.\n\nWait, let me go through it again.\n\n1. Tiger is blue (statement 8) and red (statement 9).So, the tiger is both blue and red.\n\n2. From statement 17: If something is blue, then it needs the squirrel.So, the tiger is blue, so it needs the squirrel.\n\n3. From statement 7: The squirrel needs the cow.\n\n4. From statement 18: If something needs the cow and the cow is blue, then it eats the mouse.The squirrel needs the cow, and the cow is blue (from statement 19, because the squirrel eats the mouse, so the squirrel is blue, but wait, no, statement 19 says if something eats the mouse, then it is blue.So, the squirrel eats the mouse, so the squirrel is blue.\n\nWait, so the squirrel is blue.\n\nTherefore, the statement "The squirrel is not blue" is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but let me make sure.\n\nSo, the squirrel is blue because it eats the mouse (statement 19).So, the squirrel is blue.\n\nTherefore, the statement "The squirrel is not blue" is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought the squirrel was orange, but that was a mistake because I misapplied statement 19.\n\nSo, the correct conclusion is that the squirrel is 