final_output:  ['Wait, but point 19 says if someone ignores the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog. Wait, no, point 19 says if someone visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog. So, if someone ignores the mouse, does that mean the mouse does not need the dog? Or is it the opposite?\n\nWait, point 19 is: If someone visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog. So, if someone visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog. But if someone ignores the mouse, does that mean the mouse does not need the dog? Or is it unrelated?\n\nI think it\'s unrelated. So, the fact that the rabbit ignores the mouse doesn\'t directly affect whether the mouse needs the dog. So, maybe my earlier reasoning was incorrect.\n\nLet me try again. The mouse visits the dog (point 9). So, according to point 19: If someone visits the mouse, then the mouse needs the dog. But in this case, the mouse is visiting the dog, not the other way around. So, point 19 doesn\'t apply here.\n\nSo, maybe the mouse doesn\'t need the dog. But how can we be sure?\n\nLooking at point 17: If the mouse visits the lion and the mouse needs the dog, then the dog needs the lion. We know the mouse visits the dog, but does the mouse visit the lion? Point 9 says the mouse visits the dog, but nothing about visiting the lion. So, unless the mouse also visits the lion, point 17 doesn\'t apply.\n\nPoint 18: If the rabbit needs the lion, then the lion needs the mouse. But we don\'t know if the rabbit needs the lion. The rabbit ignores the dog (point 11), but nothing about needing the lion.\n\nPoint 16: If someone likes the dog, then the dog ignores the rabbit. The mouse likes the dog (point 7), so the dog should ignore the rabbit. So, the dog ignores the rabbit. Then, according to point 15: If someone ignores the rabbit, then the rabbit ignores the mouse. So, the rabbit ignores the mouse. Now, if the rabbit ignores the mouse, does that affect whether the mouse needs the dog?\n\nPoint 14: If someone is square and dislikes th