false.\n\nWait, so earlier I thought Bob was not smart, but now I\'m seeing that he is smart because he\'s rough and big.\n\nSo where did I go wrong before? I think I confused the terms. Let me clarify.\n\nPoint 18 says: All rough, big people are smart. So if someone is rough and big, they are smart. Bob is rough and big, so he must be smart.\n\nTherefore, Bob is smart, making the statement "Bob is not smart" false.\n\nSo the correct answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought Bob was not smart because he\'s smooth and small, but that was a mistake because Bob is big, not small.\n\nWait, no, point 14 says all blue people are big, so Bob is big. So he\'s not small. Therefore, point 18 applies because he\'s rough and big, making him smart.\n\nSo the correct conclusion is that Bob is smart, so the statement "Bob is not smart" is false.\n\nTherefore, the answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B) False\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. **Bob\'s Attributes**: Bob is blue, kind, and red.\n2. **Blue People**: All blue people are big (Point 14). Therefore, Bob is big.\n3. **Big People**: Big people are kind (Point 13). This is consistent with Bob being kind.\n4. **Kind People**: All kind people are rough (Point 17). Therefore, Bob is rough.\n5. **Rough and Big People**: All rough, big people are smart (Point 18). Since Bob is both rough and big, he must be smart.\n\nThus, the statement "Bob is not smart" is false.\n\nThe correct option is: B) False', '']
