urry (fact 17).\n\nFurry and nice \u2192 cold (fact 11).\n\nCold \u2192 big (fact 19).\n\nSo yes, Anne is big. Therefore, the statement \"Anne is not big\" is false.\n\nWait, but in the initial reasoning, I thought she was small, but that was based on a different set of facts that might not apply here. Let me make sure.\n\nWait, in the initial problem, the facts are about being big, blue, cold, etc., but the terms like \"mean,\" \"foolish,\" \"slim,\" \"warm,\" and \"small\" are not part of the given facts. I think I confused the problem with another one. Let me correct that.\n\nSo, focusing on the given facts:\n\nAnne is nice \u2192 smart (14).\n\nSmart \u2192 blue (16).\n\nBlue and smart \u2192 furry (17).\n\nFurry and nice \u2192 cold (11).\n\nCold \u2192 big (19).\n\nTherefore, Anne is big. So the statement \"Anne is not big\" is false.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any contradiction. For example, is there any rule that says someone can't be both big and something else? Let's see.\n\nFrom fact 13: All blue, cold people are big. So if someone is blue and cold, they are big. Anne is blue (from 16) and cold (from 11), so she must be big.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Anne is not big\" is false. So the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B) False\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. **Anne is nice** (given).\n2. **If someone is nice, they are smart** (fact 14). Therefore, Anne is smart.\n3. **All smart people are blue** (fact 16). Therefore, Anne is blue.\n4. **Blue, smart people are furry** (fact 17). Therefore, Anne is furry.\n5. **If someone is furry and nice, they are cold** (fact 11). Since Anne is furry and nice, she is cold.\n6. **Cold people are big** (fact 19). Therefore, Anne is big.\n\nSince Anne is big, the statement \"Anne is not big\" is false.\n\nThe correct option is: B) False",
