he cow visits the lion because it needs the lion (statement 12 and 10).\n\nSo, the cow visits the lion.\n\nBut that's a separate point.\n\nNow, going back to the lion visiting itself.\n\nFrom statement 6: Lion needs bear.\n\nFrom statement 9: Lion needs lion.\n\nFrom statement 10: Lion visits lion.\n\nTherefore, the statement is true.\n\nBut wait, let me check if the lion visiting itself causes any contradictions.\n\nFor example, statement 11: If someone visits the lion and the lion is round, then they like the lion.\n\nIf the lion visits itself, and the lion is round, then the lion likes itself.\n\nBut we don't know if the lion is round.\n\nEarlier, we deduced that the lion is not round to avoid the contradiction with the bear liking the lion.\n\nSo, if the lion is not round, then statement 11 doesn't apply.\n\nTherefore, the lion visiting itself doesn't necessarily lead to liking itself, which is fine.\n\nAnother point: statement 13: If someone likes the lion and they need the lion, then the lion visits the mouse.\n\nThe mouse likes the lion (statement 7), but we don't know if the mouse needs the lion.\n\nIf the mouse needs the lion, then the lion visits the mouse.\n\nBut that's a separate point.\n\nSo, in conclusion, based on the given rules, the lion visits the lion because it needs the bear, which leads to needing the lion, which leads to visiting the lion.\n\nTherefore, the statement is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A) True\n\nThe lion visits the lion because it needs the bear, which leads to needing the lion, and thus visiting the lion according to the given rules.",
