d us somewhere.But I don\'t see a direct link to the lion visiting itself.\n\nAnother angle: statement 17 says if someone visits the cat and doesn\'t visit the bald eagle, then they are kind.The cat visits the lion, so the subject is the cat.Does the cat visit the bald eagle?We don\'t know.If the cat doesn\'t visit the bald eagle, then according to statement 17, the cat would be kind.But statement 3 says the cat is not kind.So, this creates a contradiction.Therefore, the cat must visit the bald eagle to avoid being kind, which contradicts statement 3.\n\nWait, that\'s an important point.Let me break it down:\n\n- The cat visits the lion (statement 5).\n- If the cat visits the cat (which it doesn\'t, but the subject is someone who visits the cat), but wait, statement 17 is about someone who visits the cat.So, the subject is someone else, not the cat itself.So, the cat is the one being visited, not the visitor.\n\nWait, no, statement 17 says "If someone visits the cat and they do not visit the bald eagle, then they are kind."So, the subject is someone who visits the cat.The cat is the one being visited.So, the visitor is someone else, not the cat.\n\nSo, the cat is visited by someone.Who visits the cat?From statement 5, the cat visits the lion, but the lion does not visit the cat (statement 10).So, the only visitor to the cat is the cat itself?Or maybe someone else.\n\nWait, no, the cat is visited by someone else.Let\'s see:\n\n- The lion likes the cat (statement 9), but the lion does not visit the cat (statement 10).So, the lion doesn\'t visit the cat.\n\n- The rabbit likes the bald eagle (statement 13), but does the rabbit visit the cat?Not mentioned.\n\n- The bald eagle likes the rabbit (statement 2), but does the bald eagle visit the cat?Not mentioned.\n\nSo, the only visitor to the cat is the cat itself?That doesn\'t make sense.Or perhaps the cat is visited by someone else, but we don\'t have information about that.\n\nWait, maybe the cat is visited by the lion, 