the facts, the dog eats the bear (fact 7).So, the dog eats the bear.Now, does the dog see the dog?Well, the dog sees the cat (fact 10), but does it see itself?That\'s unclear.Alternatively, maybe the dog sees the dog in the sense that it\'s aware of itself, but that\'s a stretch.\n\nWait, perhaps the conditional is about someone else who eats the bear and sees the dog.Let\'s see who else eats the bear.The cat eats the bear (fact 4), and the dog eats the bear (fact 7).So, both the cat and the dog eat the bear.\n\nNow, for the conditional: If someone eats the bear and they see the dog, then the dog is big.\n\nSo, let\'s check for the cat and the dog.\n\nFirst, the cat eats the bear (fact 4).Does the cat see the dog?From the facts, the cat sees the bear (fact 6), but I don\'t see a fact that says the cat sees the dog.So, the cat doesn\'t necessarily see the dog.Therefore, the conditional doesn\'t apply to the cat.\n\nNext, the dog eats the bear (fact 7).Does the dog see the dog?Again, the dog sees the cat (fact 10), but does it see itself?That\'s unclear.If we assume that the dog doesn\'t see itself, then the conditional doesn\'t apply.But if we consider that the dog is aware of itself, then perhaps it does see itself, which would trigger the conditional, making the dog big.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the conditional is about someone else seeing the dog.For example, the bear sees the dog (fact 3).So, the bear sees the dog.But the bear doesn\'t eat the bear, so that doesn\'t apply.\n\nWait, the bear is nice (fact 1), and the bear sees the dog (fact 3).But the bear doesn\'t eat the bear, so the conditional about eating the bear and seeing the dog doesn\'t apply to the bear.\n\nSo, perhaps the only way the dog could be big is if the dog itself meets the condition of eating the bear and seeing the dog.Since the dog does eat the bear (fact 7), and if we assume that the dog sees itself (which is a bit of a stretch), then the dog would be big.But I\'m not sure if that\'s a valid