ger\'s color—specifically, whether it\'s red. So I need to see if any information about the tiger\'s color is hidden.\n\nLooking through the statements, I see that statement 9 says the tiger is flat. Statement 14 mentions that all cruel things are flat, and statement 5 says the mouse is cruel. So the mouse is cruel and therefore flat. But that does tell me something about the tiger\'s color.\n\nI do see some statements that mention the color of the tiger. The tiger is described as flat, but that\'s a shape, not a color. There\'s mention of red and other colors in the context provided.\n\nSo, since there\'s information about the tiger\'s color, I can determine whether it\'s red or not. Therefore, the statement "The tiger is red" is known because we have some data about the tiger\'s color.\n']]
