to be blue because fact 16 only applies to round and red things.\n\n- Therefore, Harry is flat, so he is not round.\n\nThus, the statement \"Harry is not round\" is true.\n\nWait, but let me check if there are any other implications.\n\nFact 10: All young, nice things are rough.\n\nHarry is young (7). If he were nice, he would be rough (10). But rough things are not red (15). Harry is red (6), so he cannot be rough. Therefore, Harry cannot be nice.\n\nTherefore, Harry is not nice.\n\nFact 13: Nice things are quiet. Since Harry is not nice, this doesn't apply.\n\nFact 14: All quiet things are round. If Harry were quiet, he would be round. But we've established Harry is not round, so Harry cannot be quiet.\n\nTherefore, Harry is loud.\n\nFact 11: Round, quiet things are young. Since Harry is not round and not quiet, this doesn't apply.\n\nFact 12: If Erin is young and round, then she's blue. Erin is not round (3), so this doesn't apply.\n\nSo, all other facts are consistent with Harry being flat and not round.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Harry is not round\" is true.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A) True\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Harry's Attributes:**\n   - Harry is red (Fact 6).\n   - Harry is not blue (Fact 5).\n   - Harry is young (Fact 7).\n\n2. **Applying Fact 16:**\n   - Fact 16 states: If something is round and red, then it is blue.\n   - Harry is red (Fact 6). If he were round, he would have to be blue (Fact 16). However, Harry is not blue (Fact 5). Therefore, Harry cannot be round.\n\n3. **Conclusion:**\n   - Since Harry cannot be round, he must be flat.\n   - Therefore, the statement \"Harry is not round\" is true.\n\n**Answer:** The correct option is: A) True",
