he must be smooth.\n\nPoint 13 states that smooth, rude things are square. Since Dave is smooth and rude, he should be square.\n\nPoint 16 says all square things are red. So Dave, being square, must be red.\n\nNow, let\'s check if there\'s any information about Dave\'s foolishness. The only mention of "foolish" is in point 10, which says Gary is smart. There\'s no direct information about Dave\'s foolishness. However, let\'s see if any rules can help us deduce it.\n\nPoint 11 says foolish, square things are rude. Since Dave is square and rude, does that mean he\'s foolish? Wait, the rule is that if something is foolish and square, then it\'s rude. But Dave is rude and square, but the rule doesn\'t say that all rude and square things are foolish. It only says that foolish and square things are rude. So it\'s possible that Dave is foolish, but it\'s not necessarily required. So we can\'t conclude that Dave is foolish based on this.\n\nPoint 17 says if something is rude and not warm, then it is red. We know Dave is rude and warm (from earlier deductions), so this rule doesn\'t apply to him. Therefore, we can\'t use this to determine his foolishness.\n\nLooking back, the only person mentioned as smart is Gary. There\'s no information about Dave\'s foolishness, so we can\'t definitively say whether he is foolish or not. Therefore, the statement "Dave is foolish" is unknown because there\'s no information provided about Dave\'s foolishness.\n\n']]
