ly.\n\nStatement 22: If something avoids the cat and dislikes the lion, then the cat is yellow. The mouse avoids the cat, so this does apply.\n\nStatement 23: If something is warm and yellow, then it ignores the lion. The mouse is yellow, so this does apply.\n\nIs there any other way? Maybe through the cow. The cow is warm and dislikes the lion (statement 4). The cow ignores the lion? Directly stated. The lion ignores the mouse (statement 7). The mouse ignores the cow (statement 14). So the mouse ignores the cow, which in turn dislikes the lion. But does that lead to the mouse avoiding the lion? Directly.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse\'s needs could influence its actions. The mouse ignores the cat and the cow. The cat avoids the mouse (statement 2), so the mouse loses what it needs from the cat. The cow is warm and dislikes the lion, but the mouse ignores the cow. Does the mouse avoid the cow? Directly stated.\n\nWait, statement 19: If something ignores the cow and is warm, then it is old. The mouse ignores the cow (statement 14) and is warm (statement 9), so it\'s old (statement 11). That\'s inconsistent, but helps with avoiding the lion.\n\nI\'m starting to think that there\'s no direct or indirect rule that states the mouse avoids the lion. The only mention of the mouse and the lion is that the lion ignores the mouse (statement 8), but nothing about the mouse avoiding the lion.\n\nTherefore, based on the given information, we have evidence that the mouse avoids the lion. So the statement is known.\n']] 98%|#########7| 587/600 [3:34:37<00:41,  3.16s/it]
