hese facts point to Bob being green, white, big, nice, quiet, and smart.\n\nBut wait, let\'s check if there\'s any contradiction.\n\nLooking at Erin: Erin is quiet, smart, and not white. From fact 19, Erin is quiet and smart → Erin is green. But Erin is not white. From fact 14, if Erin is green and round, she would be white. But Erin is not white, so Erin cannot be green and round. But Erin is quiet and smart, so she must be green (fact 19). Therefore, Erin is green, quiet, smart, and not white. But from fact 14, if she\'s green and round, she\'s white. But Erin is not white, so she cannot be round. So, Erin is not round.\n\nSimilarly, Fiona is green, quiet, smart. From fact 14, if Fiona is green and round, she\'s white. But Fiona is not quiet (fact 6), so she\'s not covered by fact 14. Wait, no, Fiona is quiet (fact 6). So, Fiona is green, quiet, smart. From fact 14, if she\'s green and round, she\'s white. But Fiona is not necessarily round. So, Fiona could be green, quiet, smart, and not round, hence not white. But fact 15 says if Fiona is not quiet, then she\'s white. But Fiona is quiet, so this doesn\'t apply. So, Fiona is green, quiet, smart, and not white.\n\nGary is big, green, quiet, and white. From fact 14, Gary is green and round? Wait, no, Gary is big, green, quiet, and white. So, if Gary is green and round, he\'s white. But we don\'t know if Gary is round. So, perhaps Gary is not round, but he\'s big, green, quiet, and white.\n\nWait, but fact 13 says round things are quiet. So, if Gary is round, he\'s quiet. But Gary is already quiet, so that\'s consistent. But we don\'t know if Gary is round.\n\nSo, going back to Bob: He is round, so he\'s quiet (fact 13). Quiet → smart (fact 12). Quiet and smart → green (fact 19). So, Bob is green.\n\nTherefore, the statement "Bob is green" is true.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought it was false. I must have made a mistake in my initial reasoning. Let me correct that.\n\nSo, the correct conclusion is that Bob is green, 